{
  "difficulty": "intermediate",
  "score": 6,
  "estimated_minutes": 40,
  "factors": {
    "transaction_background": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "The reviewer must distinguish Wells Fargo's petition to vacate the class-arbitration award from the earlier litigation over whether the court or arbitrator should decide class availability."
    },
    "domain_knowledge": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "Understanding paragraph 14(b) requires the limited-review standards for arbitration awards, the distinction between silence and implicit consent to class arbitration, and Missouri's rules on ambiguity and extrinsic evidence."
    },
    "defect_spread": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "Although the disputed language is in paragraph 14(b), its interpretation draws on paragraph 14(a)'s carve-outs, the absence of a class waiver, and the opinion's comparison with Sappington's later FINRA-only agreement."
    },
    "court_reasoning": {
      "level": "high",
      "note": "In lines 26–32, the court infers an ambiguity finding despite the arbitrator's express disclaimer, while separately explaining why his use of extrinsic evidence would not justify vacatur even without ambiguity."
    },
    "issue_distinctness": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "d01 and d02 describe the same ambiguity in identical paragraph 14(b) language, so the reviewer need not distinguish different substantive defects."
    }
  },
  "hardest_defect": "d01, equally with d02: the difficulty is separating the individual-versus-class ambiguity from the surrounding disputes about extrinsic evidence and arbitral authority. The court reads the arbitrator as having found ambiguity despite his contrary wording, but does not independently decide the merits of class availability.",
  "reason": "Assign this to a reviewer comfortable separating contract interpretation from deferential review of an arbitration award. The competing readings are straightforward: Wells Fargo relies on silence and bilateral wording to require individual arbitration, while the employees rely on broad coverage and the absence of a class exclusion to support implicit permission. The demanding part is tracing the court's reconstruction of the arbitrator's ambiguity finding and keeping it separate from alternative grounds for denying vacatur. Focused reading of paragraph 14, the procedural background, and the substantive-review discussion should suffice; the financial-advisor training and compensation arrangements need little attention."
}
